\id JAS Original Douay-Rheims (1582/1610)
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h James
\toc1 The Catholic Epistle of James the Apostle.
\toc2 James
\toc3 JAS
\mt1 The Catholic Epistle of James the Apostle.
\is THE ARGUMENT OF THE EPISTLE OF S. JAMES.
\ip THIS Epistle (as the rest following) is directed specially, as S. Augustine saith, against the error of only faith, which some held at that time also, by misconstruing S. Paul's words. Yea not only that, but many other errors (which then also were annexed unto it, as they are now) doth this Apostle here touch expressly.
\ip He saith therefore, that not only faith, but also good works are necessary: that not only faith, but also good works do justify: that they are acts of Religion, or service and worship of God: that to keep all the commandments of God, and so to abstain from all mortal sin, is not impossible, but necessary: that God is not author of sin, no not so much as of tentation to sin: that we must stay ourselves from sinning, with fear of our death, of the Judgement, of hell: and stir ourselves to doing of good, with our reward that we shall have for it in heaven. These points of the Catholic faith he commendeth earnestly unto us, inveighing vehemently against them that teach the contrary errors. Howbeit he doth withal admonish not to neglect such, but to seek their conversion, shewing them how meritorious a thing that is. Thus then he exhorteth generally to all good works, & dehorteth from all sin. But yet also namely to certain, & from certain: as, from acception of persons, from detraction and rash judging, from concupiscence and love of this world, from swearing: and, to prayer, to alms, to humility, confession and penance: but most copiously to patience in persecution.
\ip Now, who this James was: It is not he, whose feast the Church keepeth the 25. of July, which was \f - \ft Which James wrote this Epistle.\f* S. John's brother, and whose martyrdom we have Act. 12. but he, whom the Church worshippeth the first of May, who is called \it Frater Domini, our Lord's brother\it*, and brother to Jude, and which was the first Bishop of Hierusalem, of whom we read Act. 15. & 21. and also Gal. 2. of whose wonderful austerity and purity of life, the Ecclesiastical stories do report. \it Euseb. li. 2. c. 22. Hiero. in Catalogo.\it*
\ip Therefore as the old High-priest had power and charge over the Jews, not only in Hierusalem and Jury, but also dispersed in other Countries (as we understand Act. 9. v. 1, & 2.) so S. James likewise, being Bishop of Hierusalem, and having care not only of those Jews with whom he was resident there in Jury, but of all the rest also, writeth this Epistle, \it To the twelve tribes that are in dispersion.\it* \f - \ft \+it Ia. 1.\+it* The Church readeth\f* And in them, to all Christians universally dispersed through the world.
\ip \it Catholic Epistle.\it* The word Catholic, though in the title of this Epistle & the rest following \f - \ft The Protestants abhor the word \+it Catholic\+it*.\f* (called, the Catholic Epistles) it be not wholly in the same sense, as it is in the Creed: yet the Protestants so fear and abhor the word altogether, that in some of their Bibles they leave it clean out, although it be in the Greek, and in some they had rather translate ridiculously thus, \it The general Epistle &c.\it* whereas these are famously known and specified in antiquity, by the name \f - \ft \+it Euseb. li. 2. hist. c. 22.\+it*\f* of Catholic Epistles, for that they are written to the whole Church, not to any peculiar people or person, as S. Paul's are.
\is THE CATHOLIC EPISTLE OF JAMES THE APOSTLE.
\ip The Church readeth these Catholic or Canonical Epistles in order at Matins, from the 4. Sunday after Easter until Whitsunday.
\ip \f - \ft The Protestants abhor the word \+it Catholic\+it* .\f* \it Catholic Epistle.\it* The word Catholic, though in the title of this Epistle & the rest following (called, the Catholic Epistles) it be not wholly in the same sense, as it is in the Creed: yet the Protestants so fear and abhor the word altogether, that in some of their Bibles they leave it clean out, although it be in the Greek, and in some they had rather translate ridiculously thus, \it The general Epistle &c.\it* whereas these are famously known and \f - \ft \+it Euseb. li. 2. hist. c. 22.\+it*\f* specified in antiquity, by the name of Catholic Epistles, for that they are written to the whole Church, not to any peculiar people or person, as S. Paul's are.
\c 1
\cd We have to rejoice in persecution (but if we be patient, and withal abstain from all mortal sin) 9. considering how we shall be exalted and crowned for it, when the persecutor (who enricheth himself with our spoils) shall fade away. 13. But if any be tempted to fall, or to any other evil, let him not say, God is the author of it, who is the author of all good only. 19. Such points of the Cath. faith we must be content to learn without contradiction and anger, and to do accordingly. 26. Because otherwise we may talk of Religion, but indeed it is no Religion.
\p
\v 1 \sc James\sc* the servant of God and of our Lord \sc Jesus\sc* Christ, to the twelve tribes that are in dispersion, greeting.
\v 2 \f 1 \fr 1.2 \ft The Epistle for some Martyrs.\f* Esteem it, my brethren, all joy, when you shall fall into diverse tentations:
\v 3 knowing that \x - \xt Ro. 5, 3.\x* the probation of your faith worketh patience.
\v 4 And let patience have a perfect work: that you may be perfect & entire, failing in nothing.
\v 5 But if any of you lack wisdom, let him ask of God who giveth to all men aboundantly, and upbraideth not: and it shall be given him.
\v 6 But \x - \xt Mat. 21, 22. Mar. 11, 24.\x* let him ask in faith nothing doubting. For he that doubteth, is like to a wave of the sea, which is moved & carried about by the wind.
\v 7 Therefore let not that man think that he shall receive any thing of our Lord.
\v 8 Aman double of mind is inconstant in all his ways.
\v 9 But let the humble brother glory, in his exaltation:
\v 10 and the rich, in his humility, because \x - \xt Ps. 102, 15. Eccl. 14, 18. Esa. 4, 6. 1. Pet. 1, 24. Iob 5, 17.\x* as the flower of grass shall he pass:
\v 11 for the sun rose with heat, & parched the grass, and the flower of it fell away, and the beauty of the shape thereof perished: so the rich man also shall wither in his ways.
\v 12 \f 1 \fr 1.12 \ft The Epistle for Martyrs Bishops.\f* Blessed is the man that suffereth tentation: for when he hath been proved, he shall receive the crown of life, which God hath promised to them that love him.
\v 13 Let no man when he is tempted, say that he is tempted of God. For God is not a tempter of evils, and he tempteth no man.
\v 14 But \f 1 \fr 1.14 \ft The ground of tentation to sin, is our concupiscence, & not God.\f* every one is tempted of his own concupiscence abstracted and allured.
\v 15 Afterward concupiscence when it hath conceived, bringeth forth sin. But sin when it is consummate, engendereth death.
\v 16 \f 1 \fr 1.16 \ft The Epistle on the 4. Sunday after Easter.\f* Do not err therefore, my dearest brethren.
\v 17 Every best gift, and every perfect gift, is from above, descending from the Father of lights, with whom is no transmutation, nor shadowing of alteration.
\v 18 Voluntarily hath he begotten us by the word of truth, that we may be some beginning of his creature.
\v 19 You know, my dearest brethren. And \x - \xt Prov. 17, 27.\x* let every man be swift to hear, but slow to speak, and slow to anger.
\v 20 For the anger of man worketh not the justice of God.
\v 21 For the which thing casting away all uncleanness and aboundance of malice, in meekness receive the engrafted word, which is able to save your souls.
\v 22 \x - \xt Mat. 7, 21. Ro. 2, 13.\x* But \f 2 \fr 1.22 \ft The Epistle on the 5. Sunday after Easter.\f* be doers of the word, and not hearers only, deceiving yourselves.
\v 23 For if a man be a hearer of the word, and not a doer: he shall be compared to a man beholding the countenance of his nativity in a glass.
\v 24 For he considered himself, and went his way, and by and by forgat what an one he was.
\v 25 But he that hath looked in the law of perfect liberty, and hath remained in it, not made a forgetful hearer, but a doer of the work; this man shall be \f 1 \fr 1.25 \ft Beatitude or salvation consisteth in well working.\f* blessed in his deed.
\v 26 And if any man think himself to be religious, not bridling his tongue, but seducing his heart, this man's religion is vain.
\v 27 Religion clean and unspotted with God and the Father, is this, to visit pupils and widows in their tribulation: and to keep himself unspotted from this world.
\c 2
\cd Against acception of persons. 10. From all and every sin we must abstain, having in all our words and deeds, the Judgement before our eyes: wherein works of mercy shall be required of us, 14. and only faith shall not avail us. 18. And that the Catholic by his works sheweth that he hath faith: whereas; the Heretic hath no more faith than the Devil, talk he of faith never so much, and of justification thereby only, by the example of Abraham Ro. 4. For Abraham indeed was justified by works also, 25. and likewise Rehab.
\p
\v 1 MY \x - \xt Lev. 19, 15. Deu. 1, 16. Pro. 24, 23. Eccl. 42, 1.\x* brethren, Have not the faith of our Lord \sc Jesus\sc* Christ of glory in acception of persons.
\v 2 For if there shall enter into your assembly a man having a golden ring in goodly apparel, and there shall enter in a poor man in homely attire,
\v 3 and you have respect to him that is clothed with the goodly apparel, and shall say to him, Sit thou here well: but say to the poor man, Stand thou there, or sit under my foot-stool:
\v 4 do you not judge with yourselves, and are become judges of unjust cogitations?
\v 5 Hear, my dearest brethren: hath not God chosen the poor in this world, rich in faith, and heirs of the Kingdom which God hath promised to them that love him?
\v 6 But you have dishonoured the poor man. Do not the rich oppress you by might: and themselves draw you to judgements?
\v 7 Do not they blaspheme the good name that is invocated upon you?
\v 8 If not-withstanding you fulfill the royal law according to the Scriptures, \x - \xt Lev. 19, 18. Mat. 22, 39. Rom. 13.\x* \it Thou shalt love thy neighbour as thyself,\it* you do well:
\v 9 but if you accept persons, you work sin, reproved of the Law as transgressors.
\v 10 And \x - \xt Lev. 19, 37. Deu. 1, 18.\x* whosoever shall keep the whole Law, but offendeth in one, is made guilty of all.
\v 11 For he that said, Thou shalt not commit adultery, said also, Thou shalt not kill. And if thou do not commit adultery, but shall kill; thou art made a transgressor of the Law.
\v 12 So speak ye, and so do, as beginning to be judged by the law of liberty.
\v 13 For judgement without mercy to him that hath not done mercy. And mercy \f 1 \fr 2.13 \ft \+it c κατακαυχᾶται.\+it*\f* exalteth it-self above judgement.
\v 14 What shall it profit, my brethren, if a man say he hath faith, but hath not works? Shall faith be able to save him?
\v 15 And \x - \xt Io. 3, 17.\x* if a brother or sister be naked, and lack daily food,
\v 16 and one of you say to them, Go in peace, be warmed and filled; but you give them not the things that are necessary for the body; what shall it profit?
\v 17 So faith also, if it have not works, is dead in it-self.
\v 18 But some man saith, Thou hast faith, and I have works: shew me thy faith without works; and I will shew thee by works my faith.
\v 19 Thou believest that there is one God. Thou doest well: the devils also believe and tremble.
\v 20 But wilt thou know, \f 1 \fr 2.20 \ft He speaketh to all heretics that say, faith only without works doth justify, calling them vain men, and comparing them to Devils.\f* o vain man, that faith without works is \f 2 \fr 2.20 \fq idle \ft \+it dead\+it*\f* idle?
\v 21 Abraham our father was he not justified by works, \x - \xt Gen. 22, 10.\x* offering Isaac his son upon the altar?
\v 22 Seest thou that faith did work with his works: and by the works the faith was consummate?
\v 23 And the Scripture was fulfilled, saying, \x - \xt Gen. 15, 6. Rom. 4, 3. Gal. 3.\x* \it Abraham believed God, and it was reputed him to justice, and he was called the friend of God.\it*
\v 24 Do you see that by works a man is justified; & not by faith only?
\v 25 And in like manner also \x - \xt Ios. 2, 1. 18. and 6, 22.\x* Rahab the harlot, was not she justified by works, receiving the messengers, and putting them forth another way?
\v 26 For even as the body without the spirit is dead: so also faith without works is dead.
\c 3
\cd Against proud Masters and authors of Sects. 5. Of the manifold sins of the unbridled tongue. 13. The difference betwixt proud, contentious, and worldly wisdom, and that wisdom which is heavenly, peaceable, modest, and so forth.
\p
\v 1 BE \x - \xt Mat. 23, 8.\x* ye not many masters, my brethren, knowing that you receive the greater judgement.
\v 2 For in many things we offend all. \x - \xt Eccli. 14, 1. 19, 16.\x* If any man offend not in word; this is a perfect man. He is able also with bridle to turn about the whole body.
\v 3 And if we put bits into the mouths of horses that they may obey us, we turn about all their body also.
\v 4 And behold, the ships, whereas they be great, and are driven of strong winds: yet are they turned about with a little stern whither the violence of the director will.
\v 5 So the tongue also is certes a little member, and \f 1 \fr 3.5 \ft \+it c μεγαλαυχεῖ.\+it*\f* vaunteth great things. Behold how \f 2 \fr 3.5 \fq much \ft \+it little\+it*\f* much fire what a great wood it kindleth?
\v 6 And the tongue, is fire, a whole world of iniquity. The tongue is set among our members, which defileth the whole body, and inflameth the wheel of our nativity, inflamed of hell.
\v 7 For all nature of beasts & fowls & serpents & of the rest is tamed & hath been tamed by the nature of man:
\v 8 But the tongue no man can tame, an unquiet evil, full of deadly poison.
\v 9 By it we bless God and the Father, & by it we curse men which are made after the similitude of God.
\v 10 Out of the self-same mouth proceedeth blessing & cursing. These things must not be so done, my brethren.
\v 11 Doth the fountain give forth out of one hole sweet and sour water?
\v 12 Can, my brethren, the fig-tree yield grapes, or the vine, figs? So neither \f 1 \fr 3.12 \fq can the salt water yield \ft \+it can it yield salt and sweet water.\+it*\f* can the salt water yield sweet.
\v 13 Who is wise and hath knowledge among you? Let him shew by good conversation his working in mildness of wisdom.
\v 14 But if you have bitter zeal, and there be contentions in your hearts; glory not and be not liars against the truth.
\v 15 For this is not \f 1 \fr 3.15 \ft The difference betwixt the human wisdom, specially of heretics: and the wisdom of the Catholic Church & her children.\f* wisdom descending from above: but earthly, sensual, devilish.
\v 16 For where zeal and contention is, there is inconstancy, and every perverse work.
\v 17 But the wisdom that is from above, first certes is chaste; then peaceable, modest, suasible, consenting to the good, full of mercy and good fruits, not judging, without simulation.
\v 18 And the fruit of justice, in peace is sowed, to them that make peace.
\c 4
\cd By concupiscence and love of this world, we are made enemies to God: but we should rather humble us to him. punishing ourselves for our sins. 11. Against detraction, and rash judging. 13 To remember always the uncertainty of our life.
\p
\v 1 \sc From\sc* whence are wars and contentions among you? Are they not hereof: of your concupiscences which war in your members?
\v 2 You covet, & have not. You kill, and envy; and cannot obtain. You contend and war: and you have not, because you ask not.
\v 3 You ask, and receive not: because you ask amiss: that you may consume it on your concupiscences.
\v 4 Adulterers, know you not that the \x - \xt 1. Io. 2, 15.\x* friendship of this world, is the enemy of God? Whosoever therefore will be a friend of this world, is made an enemy of God.
\v 5 Or do you think that the Scripture saith in vain: \it To envy doth the spirit covet which dwelleth in you?\it*
\v 6 And \x - \xt Prov. 3, 35. 1. Pet. 5, 5.\x* giveth greater grace. For the which cause it saith, \f 2 \fr 4.6 \ft The boldness of Heretics adding here the word \+it Scripture\+it* to the text thus, \+it And the Scripture giveth greater grace.\+it*\f* \it God resisteth the proud, and giveth grace to the humble.\it*
\v 7 Be subject therefore to God, but resist the devil, and he will fly from you.
\v 8 \f 1 \fr 4.8 \ft c Free-will & man's own endeavor necessary in coming to God.\f* Approach to God, & he will approach to you. Cleanse your hands, ye sinners: and purify your hearts, ye double of mind.
\v 9 Be miserable, and mourn, & weep: let your laughter be turned into mourning; and joy, into sorrow.
\v 10 \x - \xt 1. Pet. 5, 6.\x* Be humbled in the sight of our Lord, and he will exalt you.
\v 11 \f 1 \fr 4.11 \ft c He forbiddeth detraction, evil speaking, slandering.\f*\f 2 \fr 4.11 \ft c μὴ καταλαλεῖτε ἀλλήλων.\f* Detract not one from another, my brethren. He that detracteth from his brother, or he that judgeth his brother, detracteth from the Law, and judgeth the Law. But if thou judge the Law, thou art not a doer of the Law, but a Judge.
\v 12 For there is one Lawmaker, and Judge that can destroy and deliver.
\v 13 But thou, \x - \xt Ro. 14, 4.\x* what art thou that judgest thy neighbour? Behold now you that say, Today or tomorrow we will go into that city, and there certes will spend a year, and will traffic, and make our gain
\v 14 (who are ignorant what shall be on the morrow. For what is your life? It is a vapour appearing for a little while, and afterward it shall vanish away)
\v 15 for that you should say, \f 1 \fr 4.15 \ft All promises and purposes of our worldly affairs are to be made under condition of God's good liking & pleasure:\f* If our Lord will: and, if we shall live, we will do this or that.
\v 16 But now you rejoice in your arrogancies. All such rejoicing is wicked.
\v 17 To one therefore knowing to do good, and not doing it: to him it is sin.
\c 5
\cd By the damnation to come upon the unmerciful rich, he exhorteth the persecuted to patience and by their own reward, and by examples. 12. Not to swear at all in common talks. 13. in affliction, to pray: in prosperity, to sing: in sickness, to call for the Priests, and that they pray over them and anoil them with oil: and that the sick persons confess their sins. 19. Finally, how meritorious it is, to convert the erring unto the Catholic faith, or the sinner to amendment of life.
\p
\v 1 GO to now ye rich men, weep, \f 1 \fr 5.1 \ft A fearful description of the miseries that shall befall in the next life to the unmerciful covetous men.\f* howling in your miseries which shall come to you.
\v 2 Your riches are corrupt; and your garments are eaten of moths.
\v 3 Your gold and silver is rusted; and their rust shall be for a testimony to you, and shall eat your flesh as fire. You have stored to yourselves wrath in the last days.
\v 4 Behold the hire of the work-men that have reaped your fields, which is defrauded of you, cryeth: and their cry hath entered into the ears of the Lord of Sabboth.
\v 5 You have made merry upon the earth: and in riotousness you have nourished your hearts in the day of slaughter.
\v 6 You have \f 1 \fr 5.6 \fq presented \ft \+it condemned\+it*\f* presented, and slain the just one: and he resisted you not.
\v 7 Be patient therefore, brethren, until the coming of our Lord. Behold, the husband-man expecteth the precious fruit of the earth: patiently bearing till he receive \f 1 \fr 5.7 \ft c He meaneth either fruit or rain.\f* the timely and the lateward.
\v 8 Be you also patient, and confirm your hearts: because the coming of our Lord \f 1 \fr 5.8 \fq will approach \ft \+it is at hand.\+it*\f* will approach.
\v 9 Grudge not, brethren, one against another: that you be not judged. Behold, the judge standeth before the gate.
\v 10 Take an example, brethren, of \f 1 \fr 5.10 \ft \+it exitus mali\+it*\f* labour and patience, the Prophets, which spake in the name of our Lord.
\v 11 Behold we account them blessed that have suffered. The sufferance of Job you have heard, and the end of our Lord you have seen, because our Lord is merciful and pitiful.
\v 12 But before all things, my brethren, \x - \xt Mt. 5, 34.\x* swear not, neither by heaven, nor by earth; nor other oath whatsoever. But let your talk be, yea, yea: no, no: that you fall not under judgement.
\v 13 \f 1 \fr 5.13 \ft The Epistle in a votive Mass for the sick.\f* Is any of you in heaviness? let him pray. Is he of a cheerful heart? let him sing.
\v 14 Is any man sick among you? let him bring in the priests of the Church, and let them pray over him, \x - \xt Mr. 6, 13.\x* anoiling him with oil in the name of our Lord.
\v 15 And the prayer of faith shall save the sick: and our Lord shall lift him up: and if he be in sins, they shall be remitted him.
\v 16 \f 1 \fr 5.16 \ft b The Epistle in \+it Maioribus Litanijs\+it* on S. Mark's day, and in the Rogation days.\f*\f 2 \fr 5.16 \ft b The heretics translate, \+it Acknowledge your sins. &c.\+it* So little they can abide the very word of \+it confession.\+it*\f* Confess therefore your sins one to another: and pray one for another, that you may be saved. For the continual prayer of a just man availeth much.
\v 17 \x - \xt 3. Reg. 17. Eccli. 48. Lu. 4, 25.\x* Elias was a man like unto us, passible: and with prayer he prayed that it might not rain upon the earth, and it rained not for three years and six months.
\v 18 And \x - \xt 3. Reg. 18, 41.\x* he prayed again: and the heaven gave rain, and the earth yielded her fruit.
\v 19 My brethren, if any of you shall err from the truth, and a man convert him:
\v 20 he must know that he which maketh a sinner to be converted from the error of his way, shall save his soul from death, and \f 1 \fr 5.20 \ft He that hath the zeal of converting sinners, procureth thereby mercy and remission to himself: which is a singular grace.\f* covereth a multitude of sins.
